# MASTER PROMPT: BUILD A COMPLETE, ADVANCED FULL-STACK AI CHATBOT — NASIR AI

## 1. Main Project Goal

Build a complete, advanced, production-oriented, full-stack AI chatbot web application named **Nasir AI**.

Nasir AI must be a powerful general-purpose AI assistant inspired by the usability and organization of ChatGPT, but with its own unique branding, clean interface, useful tools, secure backend, persistent database, project workspace, and expandable architecture.

This must be a real, functioning application, not a static design, frontend-only prototype, clickable mockup, demonstration project, or unfinished template.

The purpose of Nasir AI is to help students, teachers, job seekers, developers, researchers, creators, professionals, and ordinary people with their daily tasks, learning, research, writing, programming, career development, problem-solving, and productivity.

The application must be designed so that I, the owner and creator of the application, can add more AI models, tools, integrations, and capabilities through future updates without rebuilding the entire application.

**Important exclusion:** Do not build an image generator or a video generator. Do not add image-generation or video-generation tools, buttons, pages, or API routes. Focus on a highly capable text-based AI assistant and practical productivity tools.

## 2. Non-Negotiable Development Requirements

1. Build the actual application and implement its frontend, backend, database, authentication, AI integrations, and tool functionality.
2. Do not stop after creating the home page, dashboard, sidebar, or user interface.
3. Do not use fake data, simulated AI replies, hardcoded sample job listings, placeholder search results, or artificial success messages.
4. Every visible button, menu item, form, tool, upload control, and navigation element must perform its intended action.
5. Connect each feature to its real backend implementation and verify that the complete user workflow works.
6. Do not claim that a feature is operational unless the implementation and its required integrations are working.
7. If an external service requires credentials, an API key, user permission, billing activation, or additional configuration, implement the integration correctly and clearly identify the missing setup. Never replace a missing integration with fake results.
8. Use current, officially supported SDKs, APIs, model identifiers, and package versions. Avoid deprecated APIs.
9. Fix build errors, TypeScript errors, API errors, authentication problems, database issues, and broken user interactions before considering the application complete.
10. Maintain a clean, modular codebase so future updates can extend existing functionality instead of breaking it.

Do not merely describe how to build this application. Work directly on the application code and implement as much of the complete, working product as the environment and configured services permit.

## 3. Technology Stack and Architecture

Build a responsive full-stack web application using the following architecture:

- Frontend: React, TypeScript, and a modern, accessible component system.
- Backend: A secure Node.js server runtime with organized API endpoints and reusable services.
- AI engine: Integrate the Gemini API through the current supported Google GenAI SDK.
- Database: Firebase Cloud Firestore for persistent application data.
- Authentication: Firebase Authentication, supporting Google sign-in and secure account handling.
- File storage: Firebase Cloud Storage when configured and supported, with properly secured uploads and downloads.
- AI search: Integrate Google Search grounding where supported, including the source citations returned by the API.
- AI calculation tools: Use supported code execution or other appropriate computation tools when genuinely available.
- Configuration: Store API keys and secrets in the server-side secrets manager or environment variables.
- Deployment readiness: Prepare the project to run in Google AI Studio's full-stack environment and deploy using its supported deployment workflow.

Use the current supported Gemini model available to the application. Keep the model selection configurable in one central server-side configuration so it can be updated in the future.

Never expose private API keys, service account credentials, or privileged database credentials in browser code.

